# Blog post structure

Conventions for every post in `src/content/posts/`. For voice and tone, see `blog-writing-conventions.mdc`. For dual-language requirements, see `blog-dual-language.mdc`.

---

## Frontmatter schema

All fields validated by `src/content.config.ts`:

```yaml
---
title: Post title in sentence case
date: YYYY-MM-DD
category: <category-id>            # one of the files in src/content/categories/
series: <series-id>                # optional; kebab-case grouping shared with sibling posts
tags:                              # array of lowercase kebab-case strings
  - tag-one
  - tag-two
excerpt: One-paragraph summary in the same voice as the post body.
thumbnail: /content/posts/<slug>/<image-name>.png
lang: pt                           # PT files only; omit for EN
draft: false                       # optional; defaults to false
---
```

- `title` — sentence case, no trailing period.
- `excerpt` — written in the voice of the post. Used as `<meta description>` and on listings.
- `category` — required. Must match an id under `src/content/categories/`.
- `series` — optional. Kebab-case id shared across posts meant to be read as a sequence (≥2 posts). Same value in EN and PT versions.

---

## Filename and naming

- Format: `YYYY-MM-DD-kebab-case-title.mdx` (or `.md` for legacy posts without Astro components).
- Same slug for EN (`src/content/posts/<slug>.mdx`) and PT (`src/content/posts/pt/<slug>.mdx`).
- Once published, treat the URL as permanent.

---

## Date sequencing

- Posts are listed chronologically by `date`. New post dates must be after the latest existing post.
- For a series written together, use consecutive daily dates.

---

## Categories

| Category id | When to use |
|---|---|
| `this-blog` | Features, infrastructure, or meta-posts about *this specific site* |
| `developer-tooling` | Practical posts about building tools, rules, skills, agents, dev systems |
| `strategy` | Workflow philosophy, communication, leadership, planning |
| `integrations` | Wiring third-party services and APIs (Slack, Bullhorn, etc.) |
| `ai-work` | Reflective or philosophical posts on AI changing how we think and work |
| `frontend` | Frontend architecture patterns, CSS, visualizations |

Pick the category that best fits the **primary frame** of the post.

---

## Series

`series` groups posts that read better as a sequence. It is **not** the same as category — a series is a narrative arc that can cut across categories.

- Use when ≥2 posts genuinely build on each other. Don't invent a series for a one-off post.
- The id is lowercase kebab-case, shared verbatim across all posts in the series and across languages.
- Ordering is automatic by `date`.
- The first post in a new series sets the id; subsequent posts reuse it.

---

## Tags

Lowercase kebab-case. Use generously — the search page treats them as filter chips.

**Required authorship tag** (exactly one per post): `human-written`, `ai-assisted`, or `ai-generated`.

**Topic tags**: tag every technology, tool, platform, language, or AI model mentioned. Aim for 6–15 per post.

---

## Images

Every post must reference a `thumbnail` in frontmatter and include a lead `<ImageFigure>` with the same `src`.

**ImageFigure component** (requires `.mdx`):

```html
<ImageFigure alt="Descriptive prompt-ready text" src="/content/posts/<slug>/<image>.png" caption="Optional editorial caption." />
```

- `alt` — detailed enough to use as an image-generation prompt.
- `caption` — optional; adds editorial context (not a repeat of alt).

**Lead image placement**:

- **Posts without `series`**: `<ImageFigure>` goes immediately after frontmatter, before any prose.
- **Posts with `series`**: `<ImageFigure>` must come **after the first 2–3 paragraphs**, not at the top. The `SeriesList` component floats right at the top of the text block; an image placed before text gets pushed below the series box and breaks the layout. Always start with prose first.

**Storage**: `public/content/posts/<slug>/<image-name>.png` → served at `/content/posts/<slug>/<image-name>.png`.

---

## Further reading

Every post ends with `## Further reading` linking to 1–3 other posts on the blog.

- Links must be genuinely relevant. EN links use `[Title](/YYYY/MM/DD/slug)`, PT uses `[Título](/pt/YYYY/MM/DD/slug)`.
- PT version links to PT versions of the recommended posts.
